1. Scope and definitions

"Application" means original Meshable Ops code, documentation, deployment
tools, original bundled plugins and the owner's imported WeasyCanvas source
supplied under this notice, including modified, renamed, rebranded, source and
compiled versions. Identified third-party material retains its separate terms.

"Licensor" means Graham Hill as original creator and any lawful successor
holding the relevant rights. A "Licensing Maintainer" is a current maintainer
expressly appointed in writing by the Licensor to enter agreements under
section 2 within that appointment's scope. Fork maintainers, repository
administrators, commit access and contributor status alone do not confer
licensing authority.

"Internal Use" means conducting a person's or organization's own operations,
including commercial operations. Staff, contractors acting on its behalf and
customers/suppliers accessing its business records/processes may participate.
Ordinary revenue from those goods/services is permitted.

"Supported Configuration" means using settings, configuration files,
environment variables, administrative screens, templates and extension
interfaces that the Application exposes for customization. It includes the
built-in business name, logo, favicon, colours, themes and sign-in images. It
does not include editing Application source or replacing bundled assets
outside those exposed features.

"Deployment Changes" means changes to installation/deployment scripts,
manifests, container or infrastructure definitions and runtime configuration
reasonably necessary to install, build, connect, secure and run the
Application on the chosen infrastructure. Examples include paths, domains,
ports, TLS, databases, storage, secrets and resource settings. This permission
does not extend to changes to Application features, runtime source code or
bundled branding/assets, or to deployment changes beyond those reasonably
necessary to deploy the Application.

"Permitted Services" means providing infrastructure, hosting, easy installers,
automated setup, installation, Supported Configuration, Deployment Changes,
deployment management, monitoring, backups, support, training and maintenance
of an otherwise permitted deployment. Maintenance includes installing official
updates and maintaining infrastructure; it does not authorize other
Application changes. Providers may charge for these services, including a
profit margin.

"Paywalled Offering" means selling, licensing, renting or otherwise
conditioning receipt, use of, or access to the Application or its
functionality on payment for the Application itself. It includes
software-license fees, paid Application subscriptions and paid access to
Application features, whether charged directly or hidden in a bundle, and
applies to unmodified copies and derivatives alike. Charges for Permitted
Services are excluded, even when recurring, bundled or required to operate or
access a hosted deployment. Ordinary sign-in, account controls and access
permissions are not paywalls. The substance of a charge, rather than its
label, determines whether it pays for services or the Application itself.

"Plugin" means a separately identifiable extension installed through the
plugin interfaces. Repackaging or moving core Application functionality into
an extension does not qualify it for the exception in section 4.

"Covered Work" means the Application, its modifications and derivative works,
and Plugins covered by section 4. Covered features remain covered when
renamed, split into modules, moved into another package/process or presented
as premium add-ons. Independent third-party material retains its own terms as
described in sections 4 and 5.

"Fork Changes" means copyrightable modifications and additions made to the
Application while exercising this license's modification/fork permission,
including changes to bundled plugins and covered features or Plugins
originally developed as part of that fork or modified Application. Ordinary
configuration values, uploaded branding assets, business content, unchanged
third-party code and independently developed Plugins merely installed
unchanged are excluded.

"Branding Changes" means rebranding, white-labeling or changing the
Application's product identity, logos or branding assets outside Supported
Configuration. Using the customization features already exposed by the
Application is not a Branding Change. Identifying a modified version and its
author without changing product branding does not require separate permission
or imply endorsement.

"Closed-Source Offering" means distributing a Covered Work or providing users
network access to it while withholding its required Complete Source, licensing
covered changes or features on proprietary terms, or otherwise restricting the
community rights required by section 3. An enterprise or premium edition,
covered Plugin or add-on does not escape these conditions because of its name,
packaging or placement behind a service boundary.

"Complete Source" means the preferred form for modifying covered code,
including all covered changes, features and Plugins, interface definitions,
dependency manifests and scripts/material needed to build, install and run the
exact version supplied or operated. It includes covered premium/enterprise
features; only publishing the base or community edition is insufficient.
Minified, obfuscated or compiled output alone is not Complete Source.
Credentials, business records and independent system components are excluded.
Supply usable configuration examples/placeholders instead of secrets. A link
to an unrelated or outdated source tree is insufficient.

2. Community permissions, hosting and restrictions

Subject to this license, the Licensor grants a worldwide, nonexclusive,
royalty-free copyright license to inspect, run, copy, modify, fork, install,
build, package and distribute Covered Works and provide remote access under
section 3; to use Supported Configuration and make Deployment Changes; to
provide Permitted Services; and to develop Plugins under section 4. These
permissions apply to individuals, businesses, hosting providers and service
providers. No copyright ownership or trademark rights are transferred.

Hosting on AWS, Google Cloud Platform (GCP), Microsoft Azure or any other
cloud, hosting provider or on-premises infrastructure is expressly permitted.
Paying or charging for compute, storage, networking, other infrastructure or
Permitted Services does not violate this license. A provider may supply easy
or one-click installers, automate setup, package an installation and manage
and maintain a deployment for a customer, on infrastructure controlled by
either party. These services may be paid and do not by themselves require a
separate agreement. Service charges do not excuse withholding required source
or other violations.

Easy installers and installation automation may make Supported Configuration
and Deployment Changes without a separate agreement, including edits to
covered deployment tools where reasonably necessary to deploy the Application.
This installer permission is limited to deployment needs. Any other
Application changes made by an installer must independently satisfy the
community modification and source-sharing conditions below or a separate
written agreement. Building or packaging unchanged Application code,
installing official updates and using the built-in customization features are
permitted.

Community modifications, bug fixes, new features and forks, including changes
to bundled plugins, do not require a separate agreement if you comply with
this license, including section 3's source-sharing and upstream grant-back
conditions. This permission applies to individuals and companies of every
size. It does not authorize Branding Changes or an exception to source
sharing. Private changes that are neither distributed nor made available to
users over a network do not require public disclosure solely because they were
made; the upstream grant and source-delivery duty in section 3.1 still apply.

You may not make a Paywalled Offering, a Closed-Source Offering or Branding
Changes without a separate written agreement from the Licensor or a Licensing
Maintainer acting within their authority. Service payments and the paid-Plugin
allowance do not grant an exception to these restrictions. A premium
Application edition must not withhold covered features or their source from
the community license merely because its author wrote those additions
independently.

An agreement must expressly identify each exception it grants. Permission for
paid Application access does not waive source-sharing obligations, and
permission for Branding Changes does not authorize a closed-source or
paywalled offering. Only an agreement expressly permitting proprietary
licensing or identified exceptions to section 3 may waive those conditions for
the rights, versions and uses it covers. Preserving notices, providing source,
donating or contributing upstream does not itself grant an exception. An
agreement may specify fees, development/maintenance contributions, covered
versions and other terms.

A separately executed agreement may establish alternative licensing terms for
identified Covered Works, modifications and derivative versions, including
their permitted use, distribution and sublicensing. To the extent expressly
stated in that agreement, its terms replace or supplement the corresponding
community conditions for the parties, rights, versions and uses it identifies.
All other applicable conditions continue. Such terms are effective only as
agreed by the Licensor or a Licensing Maintainer whose written appointment
covers the grant; no rights beyond that party's authority are supplied.

3. Source, notices and downstream terms

When distributing a Covered Work in source, binary, container, installer or
any other form, include this license and applicable copyright, attribution and
third-party notices, identify material modifications and provide its Complete
Source to recipients at no additional charge. License the entire covered
version, including your covered modifications, features and Plugins, under
these same community terms. An accessible download of the exact Complete
Source alongside the distribution is sufficient; an incomplete tree or a
future promise is not.

If you operate a Covered Work over a network, prominently offer every user
interacting with it the Complete Source of the exact version they use, at no
additional charge, through a customary download mechanism. This includes
hosted, managed and SaaS deployments, internal users and users of covered
Plugins and premium features, whether or not the deployed code has been
modified. Normal deployment account access may be required; no separate source
fee, NDA or additional contractual restrictions may be imposed. Keep the
source offer usable while operating that version. Source need not be published
to the general public solely because of Internal Use, but it must be available
to those users.

Recipients and network users receive the same community permissions to
inspect, copy, modify, fork and redistribute Covered Works, subject to this
license's branding and commercial restrictions. Do not replace these terms
with a proprietary EULA, impose confidentiality or non-redistribution
conditions on covered code, or use legal or technical restrictions to prevent
exercising those permissions. Do not purport to grant rights you do not hold.

Providing only the base edition's source while withholding covered extensions,
patches, backend changes or premium features does not satisfy this section.
Renaming a fork, separating a feature into a Plugin/service or requiring a
paid account does not remove source obligations for covered code. The express
exceptions in sections 4 and 5 for independent third-party material still
apply. Nothing here authorizes an otherwise prohibited paywall or Branding
Changes. Only an express written exception under section 2 can authorize a
Closed-Source Offering for the covered rights and uses.

Ordinary business records, generated documents, uploaded files, personal data
and secrets do not become covered code merely because the Application
processes them. Do not include private data or credentials in source offers.

3.1. Upstream grant-back for fork changes

As a condition of making Fork Changes under this license, you grant the
Licensor a worldwide, nonexclusive, perpetual, irrevocable, royalty-free
copyright license over all such Fork Changes you own or are authorized to
license. The Licensor may copy, use, adapt, combine, merge, publish,
distribute and sublicense them as part of Meshable Ops and its derivatives
under this community license or terms established under section 2, including
the right to authorize others to exercise those rights and to transfer the
grant with lawful project ownership. No royalty, fee, exclusivity or
obligation to merge or maintain your changes is created. You retain copyright
and your own permitted uses of your original code.

The grant takes effect when you make the Fork Changes using the permission in
this license; it is not conditional on submitting a pull request, publishing
the fork, signing a separate CLA or the project accepting your changes. You
must have authority from the relevant rights holders, including employers,
clients and contributors, to supply this grant for all incorporated Fork
Changes. Identify third-party material and do not purport to grant rights you
do not control. Unchanged third-party dependencies retain their own terms.

Within 30 days of the Licensor's written request, provide the Complete Source
of your Fork Changes and the covered build/integration material needed to use
them, at no charge and without additional restrictions. A usable link to the
exact source is sufficient. This applies to private/internal Fork Changes as
well as published forks; it does not require publishing to the general public
or disclosing credentials, business records, personal data or other excluded
material. The normal recipient/network-user source duties in section 3 also
continue to apply.

For patent claims you control that would necessarily be infringed by your Fork
Changes alone or by their combination with the Application, you also grant the
Licensor and its sublicensees a worldwide, royalty-free, perpetual patent
license to make, use, distribute and otherwise exercise the above copyright
grant. The patent-litigation termination rule in section 5 applies to a
recipient initiating such litigation. Copyright grants already made under this
section survive later termination of your community permissions or removal of
the fork.

This grant-back does not accept the separate CLA, transfer ownership or reach
independently developed Plugins outside the definition of Fork Changes. The
CLA may separately document identified contributions and additional authority.
An exception to the grant-back condition itself requires an express written
agreement from the Licensor or an authorized Licensing Maintainer.

4. Paid community plugins and integration components

Plugin authors may charge for copies, paywalled access, subscriptions,
hosting, support and services for their separately identifiable Plugins and
retain the proceeds without an automatic royalty/revenue share to the
Licensor. This express exception permits developing and modifying the
separately identifiable Plugin itself through the exposed plugin interfaces.
It does not authorize Branding Changes, a Closed-Source Offering or a
Paywalled Offering of the Application. Permitted Services and compliant
community modifications remain allowed alongside paid Plugins. Repackaging
core functionality as a Plugin or bundling Application access into a paid
Plugin cannot evade section 2.

As a condition of this Plugin permission, Application-derived extensions and
Plugins using the Application-specific plugin interfaces must be supplied
under this license, including their original code, this paid-Plugin exception
and section 3's source/notice conditions. They are Covered Works even if
separately packaged or described as premium features. Identified third-party
dependencies retain their own terms. Authors retain ownership of their
original Plugin code. A Plugin paywall cannot withdraw recipients' community
rights, charge extra for required source or withhold source from its network
users. Publishing or selling an independently developed Plugin outside the
definition of Fork Changes does not accept a CLA or grant the Licensor
relicensing rights over that Plugin. Section 3.1 applies to Plugins originally
developed as part of a fork and other Fork Changes. An identified contribution
expressly accepted under the CLA is governed by that agreement to its stated
extent.

Independently authored, separable external-service clients/adapters may retain
another license where it permits the actual combination and distribution.
Preserve its notices, source and other requirements. Independent remote
services retain their own terms. An API, ZIP, language or process boundary
alone does not exempt Application-derived code or eliminate third-party
obligations.

5. Ownership, patents and upstream material

Each author retains copyright. Each Licensor grants a nonexclusive,
royalty-free patent license, limited to claims they control necessarily
infringed by their covered contribution, to exercise the above permissions.
This patent permission terminates if you initiate patent litigation alleging
the covered work infringes a patent. Only rights owned or authorized for grant
are supplied by this license.

Upstream packages, services, images, fonts and trademarks retain their own
terms. No removal of their notices or claim of official endorsement is
authorized. A commercial agreement cannot waive third-party rights. A
proprietary exception for a combination requires sufficient rights over every
covered contribution included. Section 3.1 supplies a grant for covered Fork
Changes from their authorized rights holders. Independent Plugins and other
code outside that grant need their own adequate licensing or contributor
permission before proprietary relicensing.

6. Earlier versions and existing rights

For code controlled by the Licensor, this notice covers the current version
and earlier unreleased versions, branches and snapshots first supplied under
this notice, and later copies bearing it. A separately executed license
governs its identified versions/uses to its stated extent.

This notice does not retroactively revoke, narrow or replace a valid license
already granted to another recipient, including an earlier community license
1.0 or AGPL grant. A recipient may retain rights in a legitimately received
earlier copy under its original terms. Repository privacy alone does not
establish that no grant/disclosure occurred. This notice cannot be imposed on
independent third-party code without the necessary rights or relicensing
authority.

7. Termination and reinstatement

Permissions terminate upon material violation. They reinstate automatically if
a first violation is cured within 30 days of written notice by the relevant
Licensor, unless that Licensor has already permanently terminated your rights
for a prior violation. Other reinstatement requires written permission.
Compliant downstream recipients retain their independent permissions.
Termination does not require return of ordinary business data.

